Description: Bound every EC/PMC2 wait in time, resync after timeouts, log them
 Upstream polled each IBF/OBF status 5000 times with usleep_range(20, 50):
 100-255 ms per wait, and one fanN_input read could hold the mutex for
 about 12 s (48 waits), a pwm1/pwm4 write about 4.8 s (B1, C9; worst case
 in befunde/raw/p3-b1-worstcase-20261009.txt). Timeouts were not logged,
 so the long-term criterion K1/B11 could never trigger. After a timeout the
 next transaction only drained without waiting; a late byte could pass as
 a reading (B7 note).
 .
 Now:
 - each status wait uses read_poll_timeout() with a ktime deadline of
   100 ms (N5_WAIT_TIMEOUT_US). Rationale: the B1 limit per status wait
   and the lower bound of upstream's loop; EC response times are not
   measured, so the value is not made tighter. The status is read once
   more after the deadline (no false timeout through preemption);
 - all waits of one sysfs access share a budget of 1000 ms
   (N5_ACCESS_BUDGET_MS), which also bounds the fan tearing retries
   (still 4 attempts). Lock hold per access <= 1 s plus one resync sleep
   (<= 20 ms) and scheduling delay, below the regulator's 2 s minimum
   interval. The unbind/shutdown restore gives each touched channel its
   own budget (<= 4 s for four channels);
 - a timeout marks the interface (EC 0x62/0x66 or PMC2 0x68/0x6c); the
   next transaction there first sleeps 10-20 ms (N5_RESYNC_US), drains
   OBF and waits for IBF=0 before sending (B7);
 - every timeout is logged with dev_warn_ratelimited: status port, status
   byte, awaited flag, command and subcommand/address, and whether the
   access budget was used up. Suppressed messages are counted by the
   ratelimit ("callbacks suppressed"). The text contains "timeout", which
   K1 of the F8b watcher matches (B11/K1 observable);
 - a failed PWM write leaves the cached pwm/pwm_enable unchanged and
   returns the error (E3, error reported; this was already the case for
   pwmN and is kept for the new paths).
 .
 Decision, no automatic 255 write in the driver after a failed write:
 after a timeout the EC is most likely not answering, so a second
 multi-byte sequence would most likely fail as well and double the lock
 hold; if it succeeded it would change the channel to a state nobody
 requested, behind the regulator. The regulator already sets all channels
 to 255 and alarms after two write errors (n5-fangov controller.go). E3's
 "fan not left lower than before" is therefore not guaranteed by the
 driver: a write that fails after some curve points were sent can leave
 those points on the EC without the apply action; whether the EC uses
 them before the action is unmeasured. E3 stays open for an operator
 decision (accept the regulator mitigation, or a later patch).
 .
 Resolves B1, C9 (bounded and documented), B7, makes B11/K1 observable;
 E3 partly (see above).
 .
 Tested: build/static analysis as 0001. Not tested on hardware: normal
 operation without any timeout message (F8b run 2, 7 days), latency of
 fanN_input/pwmN writes, behaviour with an EC that does not answer
 (not injectable on the reference host without port I/O; F8c).
